Makefile: recognise ppc64le, riscv64 and s390x, and fail loudly otherwise.

PORTABILITY FIX -- WORTH SENDING UPSTREAM.

The Makefile builds a .NET RID as $(OS_ID)-$(ARCH_ID), mapping `uname -m` to
an ARCH_ID through a list of ifeq cases: x86_64, amd64, x86, arm, arm64,
aarch64. Anything else leaves ARCH_ID empty, and the RID silently becomes
"linux-". The build then fails a long way downstream with

    error NETSDK1083: The specified RuntimeIdentifier 'linux-' is not
    recognized.

which does not point at the Makefile at all.

.NET itself is not the limitation here. On this machine `dotnet --info`
reports RID: linux-ppc64le, so the runtime knows the platform perfectly well;
only marksman's arch table does not.

This adds ppc64le, riscv64 and s390x, and turns the silent-empty case into a
$(error) that names the unrecognised machine. The second half is the more
useful change: without it, the next architecture to come along gets the same
misleading NETSDK1083 rather than a message pointing at the table it needs to
be added to.

Not ppc64le-specific.

--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -60,6 +60,23 @@
 	ifeq ($(TARGET),aarch64)
 		ARCH_ID := arm64
 	endif
+
+	ifeq ($(TARGET),ppc64le)
+		ARCH_ID := ppc64le
+	endif
+
+	ifeq ($(TARGET),riscv64)
+		ARCH_ID := riscv64
+	endif
+
+	ifeq ($(TARGET),s390x)
+		ARCH_ID := s390x
+	endif
+
+	ifeq ($(ARCH_ID),)
+		$(error Unrecognised machine "$(TARGET)"; cannot build a .NET RID. \
+		        Add a case for it above.)
+	endif
 endif

 RID := $(OS_ID)-$(ARCH_ID)
